            "instructions": "Create your own small expense CSV file (for example columns: date, category, amount, note) with about 8-12 rows, including at least one deliberately malformed row (for example a missing amount, a non-numeric amount, or a row with too few fields). If you already have a suitable expense CSV, use that instead. Write a short script in the language or tool you are currently learning to: (1) read the CSV rows; (2) group the amounts by category and print each category total; (3) detect any malformed row and print a clear explanation of what is wrong with it, instead of silently skipping it. Run the script on your file and check that the totals match what you can verify by hand and that each malformed row is explained. Then change the data (add or remove a category, change some amounts, and introduce a different malformed row), save it as a second file, run the script again, and check that the new totals are correct and the new malformed row is explained. Keep both the script and the two CSV files. If you hit a specific error you cannot resolve, note the exact input, the expected result and the actual result so it can be addressed next.",
